window.CARDS_V4 = [{"module": 6, "title": "Sperling: whole and partial report", "researcher": "Sperling", "pages": [13, 15, 16, 17, 18], "blocks": [{"text": "**Sperling (1960)** asked whether people report so few letters from a quick flash because they only register a few, or because the image fades before they can say them all. He flashed arrays of three rows of four letters and compared three report methods: **whole report** (name as many as you can), **partial report** (a tone right after the display cues one row) and **delayed partial report** (the tone comes after a delay).", "children": ["The question was whether the reporting limit reflects storage or fading."]}, {"text": "In whole report people named about **4.5 of 12 letters**, roughly a third. In immediate partial report they named about **3.3 of 4 letters (82 percent)** of the cued row, for any row. Because the tone came only after the display was gone and nobody knew which row would be cued, information about all rows must have been available at first. With a delayed tone, performance fell to about one letter, and by about **1 second** it was near the whole-report level."}, {"text": "So **sensory memory has a large capacity but fast decay**. The trap is reading the whole-report score of four or five items as the size of the store, when it is a limit on reporting before the trace fades.", "children": ["Whole report measures reporting speed against fading, not capacity."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Brown–Peterson: delay and trial history", "researcher": "Brown and Peterson and Peterson", "pages": [24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30], "blocks": [{"text": "**Brown (1958)** and **Peterson and Peterson (1959)** asked how fast short-term memory fades when rehearsal is blocked. Participants saw three letters, counted backward by threes during a delay of 3 to 18 seconds, and then recalled the letters. The counting prevented ordinary maintenance rehearsal, and the researchers also tracked where each trial fell in the series."}, {"text": "On the **first trial** recall stayed high even after 18 seconds. By the **third trial** recall at 18 seconds was lower, and after many trials it fell to almost nothing. The same delay did far more damage when more earlier letters had piled up.", "children": ["Trial history, not only elapsed time, drove the forgetting."]}, {"text": "This is **proactive interference**: earlier trigrams made the new one harder to keep. The trap is the average over all trials, which looks like steady decay with time but mixes easy early trials with hard late ones. The steep drop comes mostly from the later trials, so the pattern is evidence for interference as well as decay."}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Visual change detection and complexity", "researcher": "Alvarez and Cavanagh", "pages": [37, 39, 41, 42, 43, 44], "blocks": [{"text": "**Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)** asked whether visual short-term memory capacity is set by the number of items or by the amount of information in them. They used **change detection**: people viewed a first array (S1) for 100 milliseconds, then judged whether a second display (S2), shown for 2,000 milliseconds, was the same or different. They varied the number of colored squares, what changed in colored bars (angle, color or both), and the complexity of the objects (colored squares, Chinese characters, random polygons, shaded cubes)."}, {"text": "Accuracy fell dramatically above about **four items**. Remembering two features of a bar was about as easy as remembering one. Capacity also fell as objects became more complex, from about four for colored squares to fewer than two for shaded cubes.", "children": ["Capacity depends on how much information each object carries."]}, {"text": "So visual short-term memory holds about **three to four whole objects**. The trap is carrying over Miller's seven: for visual memory the estimate is about four, and fewer for complex objects."}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Digit-span training and chess expertise", "researcher": "Ericsson and colleagues; Chase and Simon", "pages": [45, 46], "blocks": [{"text": "Can chunking and knowledge let people hold far more in short-term memory? **Ericsson and colleagues (1980)** trained a college student on digit span. By organizing the digits into familiar running times, he reached a span of about **79 digits**."}, {"text": "**Chase and Simon (1973)** tested player skill (masters versus beginners) and board type (real-game arrangement versus random arrangement) by measuring how many chess pieces people placed correctly after a brief look. Masters placed far more pieces than beginners on real-game boards, but they were no better on random boards.", "children": ["The masters' edge vanished when the familiar patterns were removed."]}, {"text": "Both results show that **chunking with knowledge from long-term memory** stretches effective capacity. Experts do not have bigger short-term memories. The trap is crediting the masters with a larger store, when it is their knowledge of real patterns that helps."}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Conrad · sound-based errors after visual presentation", "researcher": "Conrad (1964)", "pages": [8, 9], "blocks": [{"text": "**Conrad (1964)** asked whether errors in remembering seen letters are based on how the letters look or how they sound. He flashed letters briefly, half the time similar and half the time not similar, and participants wrote them down."}, {"text": "Errors more often swapped in a letter that **sounded like** the target (B for E) than one that **looked like** it (F for E). The letters entered through vision, but the confusions followed their spoken names.", "children": ["This is the phonological similarity effect."]}, {"text": "So seen letters are recoded into a **sound-based form** in working memory, through the phonological loop. The trap is treating presentation format as memory code: what people see is not the same as the form in which it is maintained."}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Word length, bilingual digit span, and articulatory suppression", "researcher": "Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984); Ellis and Hennelly (1980)", "pages": [10, 11], "blocks": [{"text": "What limits how much verbal material the **phonological loop** can hold? **Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984)** found that people remembered more short words than long words, because long words take longer to pronounce and rehearse. **Ellis and Hennelly (1980)** tested Welsh-English bilingual children and found a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English, because Welsh number names take longer to say."}, {"text": "The Welsh-English gap comes from **pronunciation time**, not from the values of the numbers. Verbal span depends on how much can be rehearsed in about **1.5 to 2 seconds**.", "children": ["Faster rehearsal keeps more items active."]}, {"text": "**Articulatory suppression**, speaking while memorizing, occupies the rehearsal process. It lowered recall and reduced both the word-length effect and the phonological similarity effect, which shows that speech-based rehearsal is used to maintain verbal information."}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Brooks · sentence and F dual-task comparisons", "researcher": "Brooks (1968)", "pages": [13, 14, 15], "blocks": [{"text": "**Brooks (1968)** asked whether a response interferes more when it uses the same kind of memory as the thing being held. He varied the response mode, speaking or pointing, across two memory tasks. In one, people memorized a sentence and judged whether each word was a noun. In the other, they memorized an F and judged whether each corner was an inside or outside corner."}, {"text": "With the **sentence**, pointing was easier than speaking, because holding the sentence used the phonological loop and speaking competed with it. With the **F**, speaking was easier than pointing, because holding the F used the visuospatial sketchpad and pointing added a spatial demand.", "children": ["Interference depends on the combination of tasks."]}, {"text": "The results reverse, so neither speaking nor pointing is easier overall. The pattern supports some independence between verbal and visual or spatial memory."}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Shepard and Metzler · angle and mental-rotation time", "researcher": "Shepard and Metzler (1971)", "pages": [17], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people mentally rotate objects, and does more rotation take more time? **Shepard and Metzler (1971)** showed pairs of block-built objects at different orientations and measured the reaction time to judge whether the two objects matched. The rotation could be in the picture plane or in depth."}, {"text": "For picture-plane pairs, reaction time **increased as the angle increased**, approximately linearly. A larger angle needs a larger mental transformation before the objects can be compared.", "children": ["This points to an active operation on a spatial representation, not storage of an unchanged picture."]}, {"text": "The trap is mixing up the two kinds of turning: picture-plane rotation turns within the flat plane, while depth rotation turns in three-dimensional space."}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Vogel · relevant load, distractors, and filtering efficiency", "researcher": "Vogel and colleagues (2005)", "pages": [20, 21, 22], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people with higher working-memory capacity keep irrelevant items out better? **Vogel and colleagues (2005)** first measured capacity to separate higher- and lower-capacity participants. They then recorded an event-related potential (ERP), a memory-related brain response, during a 900 millisecond retention interval, while people judged whether a relevant orientation had changed. Displays had two relevant red bars, four relevant red bars, or two red bars plus two irrelevant blue bars."}, {"text": "High-capacity people showed a larger response for four items than for two, and **little change** when blue distractors were added. For low-capacity people, adding distractors raised the response toward the four-item level, so irrelevant material entered working memory. Capacity correlated **positively** with filtering efficiency.", "children": ["Better filtering goes with higher capacity."]}, {"text": "So capacity depends on which items get in as well as how many targets there are. The trap is the direction of the signal: a bigger response means a stronger negative deflection, not a more positive voltage."}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Köhler · circle representation and equal diagonals", "researcher": "Köhler", "pages": [3], "blocks": [{"text": "**Köhler** asked whether reorganizing how a problem is represented can make a hidden relationship usable. He used a circle problem: given that the radius is **r**, find the length of line **x**. This is a demonstration, not a comparison of groups, and what matters is how the figure is represented.", "children": ["Restructuring the representation, not new calculation, makes the answer available."]}, {"text": "The black line x looks like an isolated unknown inside the circle, and treating it that way gets nowhere. The key is to see x as one **diagonal of a rectangle** whose lower-right corner sits at the circle's center. The dashed line from the upper-left corner on the circle to the center is a radius, and it is the **other diagonal** of the same rectangle. The two diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so **x = r**.", "children": ["Spotting the rectangle with equal diagonals is what solves it."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Metcalfe and Wiebe · insight puzzles and warmth judgments", "researcher": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)", "pages": [5, 6, 7], "blocks": [{"text": "**Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)** asked whether people feel gradually closer to a solution on insight problems, the way they do on methodical ones. They compared **insight problems** (the triangle and chain problems) with **noninsight problems** (algebra). Every **15 seconds**, participants gave a **warmth rating** of how close they felt, where hot means close to the solution and colder means far."}, {"text": "On algebra, warmth **rose gradually** as the solution approached, because each step brought the answer visibly nearer. On insight problems, warmth **stayed low until close to the solution and then jumped sharply**, from about **3 to 7**. People were poor at predicting how near they were, because a change in representation can make the answer appear without growing feelings of progress.", "children": ["Insight comes with a sudden change in felt progress, unlike methodical solving."]}, {"text": "Not all problem solving is sudden. The sharp jump describes insight problems, not algebra."}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Candle problem · matchbox presentation and functional fixedness", "researcher": "", "pages": [8], "blocks": [{"text": "This study asked whether seeing an object only in its familiar use blocks using it for something else. Participants had a candle, matches in their box, and tacks, and had to mount the candle on a vertical corkboard so it burned without dripping wax on the floor. The researchers varied whether the matches were **inside the box** or the matchboxes were **empty**."}, {"text": "The solution is to tack the matchbox to the board and use it as a **platform** for the candle. Participants given **empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve it**. When the box held matches, people saw it only as a container, which is **functional fixedness**, a restriction of an object to its familiar function.", "children": ["The objects provided were enough to solve it. The obstacle was the familiar use of the box."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Two-string problem · pliers as a weight and a movement hint", "researcher": "", "pages": [9, 10], "blocks": [{"text": "This study asked what helps people see a new use for an object in the **two-string problem**. Two strings hang too far apart to hold both at once, and participants had to tie them together. The researchers manipulated whether an experimenter **'accidentally' set a string in motion**, which served as a hint."}, {"text": "The solution is to tie the **pliers** to one string as a weight so it swings like a pendulum, then catch it while holding the other string. The pliers' useful property is their weight, not their ability to grip, and the familiar grip function is the functional fixedness. After the swinging hint, **most participants solved the problem**.", "children": ["The hint restructured the representation, so the string became something that could be swung within reach."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Water-jug problems · practice and a simpler available solution", "researcher": "", "pages": [11], "blocks": [{"text": "This demonstration asks whether a method that worked earlier can block a simpler solution. Participants get three empty jugs with given capacities and must reach a target amount. Early problems are all solved by the same formula, **B − A − 2C**, for example 127 − 21 − 3 − 3 = 100. Nothing is measured with participants here. The demonstration traces which method each problem allows."}, {"text": "Repeated success with that formula builds a **mental set**. In **problem 7** (A 15, B 39, C 3, goal 18), the familiar method works, but **A + C** gives 18 directly. In **problem 8** (A 28, B 59, C 3, goal 25), **A − C** gives 25 directly. The familiar method is still correct in both, so it is brought first.", "children": ["The mental set inhibits the simpler solution, not the correct answer."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Search demonstrations · tile puzzle and Tower of Hanoi subgoals", "researcher": "Newell and Simon", "pages": [13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18], "blocks": [{"text": "**Newell and Simon** described solving as a **search through a problem space**, and the demonstrations here trace that idea with a numbered-tile puzzle and the Tower of Hanoi. Nothing was varied and no participants were measured. **Operators** set which moves are legal, routes pass through intermediate states, and a **search tree** shows the branches of alternative states."}, {"text": "In the Tower of Hanoi, **means-end analysis** reduces the difference between the current state and the goal by achieving **subgoals**. The first subgoal frees the large disk, the second frees the third peg, and the third moves the large disk onto it. A subgoal is an intermediate condition that makes a move possible, not the final answer, and the smaller disks still have to be brought over afterward.", "children": ["Different subgoals remove different obstacles to the same move."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Mutilated checkerboard · labels, pairing, and think-aloud protocols", "researcher": "", "pages": [19, 20, 21, 22], "blocks": [{"text": "This study asked whether how a problem is stated changes how hard it is to find the **pairing constraint**. With two opposite corners removed, 62 squares remain, which matches the area of 31 dominoes. But the removed corners share a color, and every domino covers one square of each color, so the covering is impossible. Equal area is not enough."}, {"text": "The researchers varied the information on the board: **blank, colored, labeled black and pink, or labeled bread and butter**. They measured how fast participants solved the problem and collected **think-aloud reports**. Participants in the **bread-and-butter** condition solved it **twice as fast** as those with the blank board, because bread and butter come in pairs and point to one of each kind per domino.", "children": ["Labels that point to the right representation cut solving time."]}, {"text": "Think-aloud reports showed thinking shift from squares as positions to the **relation between the two kinds of square**, matching the Gestalt idea of restructuring."}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Marriage and checkerboard · mapping a shared pairing constraint", "researcher": "", "pages": [3, 4, 5], "blocks": [{"text": "This pair of problems asks whether a solver can see why 31 pairs are impossible when the two groups are unequal. In the **Russian marriage problem**, 32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women become 30 men and 32 women after two bachelors die, and the question is whether 31 heterosexual marriages can be formed. The matching board problem removes two same-colored corners and asks whether 31 dominoes can cover it."}, {"text": "The answer to both is **no**. Each marriage needs one man and one woman, so with 30 men **at most 30 marriages** are possible. Each domino needs one square of each color, so the same imbalance blocks the board. The total count of 62 says 31 pairs, but the pairing rule decides.", "children": ["The solution comes from the one-of-each relationship, not total size or area."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Duncker and Gick–Holyoak · radiation, fortress, and reminders", "researcher": "Duncker; Gick and Holyoak", "pages": [6, 7, 8, 9, 10], "blocks": [{"text": "**Duncker's** radiation problem set a tumor that needs high-intensity rays against healthy tissue that only survives low intensity. **Gick and Holyoak** asked whether people notice and use a solved source story, the **fortress** story, to reach the answer of several weak rays **converging** on the tumor. They varied whether participants read the fortress story first, and whether they were told to think about it."}, {"text": "Only about **10%** solved radiation without the analogy. After reading the fortress story, about **30%** solved it. When told to think about the story, about **75%** did. Having a useful source in memory is not enough, because people must **notice** it and map it onto the target.", "children": ["Many people read the source and do not spontaneously use it. The reminder is the difference between 30% and 75%."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Lightbulb transfer · surface and structural similarities", "researcher": "", "pages": [11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16], "blocks": [{"text": "This study asked whether a source with similar **surface features**, or similar **structure**, helps solve a target problem. Participants knew one of three sources: the radiation problem, a **fragile-glass** lightbulb problem, or an **insufficient-intensity** lightbulb problem. A control group had no source. The researchers measured whether participants solved the target."}, {"text": "**81%** who knew radiation solved the lightbulb problem, versus **10%** of controls. For radiation as the target, **69%** who read the fragile-glass solution solved it, versus **33%** after the insufficient-intensity version. Both lightbulb sources use converging lasers, but only the fragile-glass one shares the reason, damage to the surroundings.", "children": ["Matching structure, a strong effect at the target plus protected surroundings, helps more than matching only the objects."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "In vivo observation · analogies in scientific work", "researcher": "", "pages": [17], "blocks": [{"text": "This work asked how often real scientists use analogies during ordinary work. Nothing was manipulated. Researchers **observed scientists in real-world laboratory meetings** and counted the analogies used in a one-hour meeting."}, {"text": "Scientists use about **3 to 15 analogies** in a one-hour laboratory meeting. Analogies are common in real work even though they are hard to transfer in laboratory tasks, which is the **analogical paradox**.", "children": ["In vivo research gives real context but little control of variables, and it can be time-consuming."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Chase and Simon · game positions, random layouts, and chunks", "researcher": "Chase and Simon (1973)", "pages": [19], "blocks": [{"text": "**Chase and Simon (1973)** asked whether expert chess recall comes from a general memory advantage or from organized knowledge. They compared a **chess master** with a **beginner with under 100 hours** of experience, and used either **actual game positions** or **random arrangements**. Each person viewed the board for **five seconds** and then reproduced the piece positions."}, {"text": "The expert did much better than the beginner on actual game positions, because long-term memory made meaningful arrangements available as **chunks**. For random arrangements, those familiar chunks were not available. Expert memory therefore depends on chunks stored in long-term memory, not on a general memory advantage.", "children": ["Experts do not remember everything better. The advantage is tied to meaningful positions."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Chi and colleagues · surface-based and principle-based sorting", "researcher": "Chi et al. (1982)", "pages": [20, 21], "blocks": [{"text": "**Chi and colleagues (1982)** asked whether experts and novices organize physics problems in the same way. The experts were **physics professors** and the novices were students with **one semester** of physics. Each group sorted **24 physics problems** into groups by similarity."}, {"text": "**Novices grouped by surface features**, such as inclined planes, while **experts grouped by structural features**, such as conservation of energy. The same problem 24 pairs with problem 23 for a novice, through inclined planes, but with problem 21 for an expert, through conservation of energy.", "children": ["Expertise changes which relationship is the basis for similarity."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Availability judgments · letter positions and causes of death", "researcher": "", "pages": [6, 7, 8], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people judge how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind? In one test, people were asked whether English has more words that begin with **r** or more words with **r as the third letter**. In another, they were given **paired causes of death**, such as botulism versus asthma, and chose which was more frequent or more likely.", "children": ["The question probes the **availability heuristic**: easy recall is treated as a sign of high frequency."]}, {"text": "About **70%** said more words begin with r, yet words with r in the third position are actually **three times more common**. Words are easier to retrieve by first letter, so those words feel more numerous. For causes of death, many people picked the dramatic cause over the more common one such as asthma, because a botulism death can become front-page news while asthma deaths go virtually unnoticed.", "children": ["Memorable or newsworthy does not mean common."]}, {"text": "Ease of remembering serves as a cue for probability, but it is shaped by distinctiveness and emotional impact as well as by how often something really happens."}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Representativeness · Robert, Jack, and Linda", "researcher": "", "pages": [9, 10, 11], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people rely on resemblance instead of base rates and probability rules? Participants read a description and judged occupation or category. **Robert** wears glasses, speaks quietly and reads a lot, and many people call him a **librarian** rather than a farmer, even though there are about **2.2 million farms** and only **120,000 libraries**.", "children": ["A good fit with the stereotype pushed the base rate out of the judgment."]}, {"text": "In the **lawyer-engineer problem**, the group has **70 lawyers and 30 engineers**. With no description, people correctly use the 70/30 base rate. Once **Jack** is described as conservative, careful and fond of home carpentry and math puzzles, they ignore the base rate and say he is an engineer.", "children": ["Base rates are used when they are all there is, and dropped once a description is available."]}, {"text": "In the **Linda problem**, about **82%** chose that Linda is a bank teller and active in the feminist movement over bank teller alone. Feminist bank tellers are a **subset** of bank tellers, so the combination can never be more probable than the single event.", "children": ["The better-fitting story won over the nested-set logic of the **conjunction rule**."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Hospital samples · extreme proportions and sample size", "researcher": "", "pages": [12, 13, 14], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people take sample size into account when judging how often extreme proportions occur? Participants compared a larger hospital with about **45 births a day** and a smaller hospital with about **15 births a day**. About half of all babies are boys, and they chose which hospital recorded more days on which **over 60% were boys**: the larger, the smaller, or about the same.", "children": ["The question is about how often a day is extreme, not about the total number of boys born."]}, {"text": "The most common answer, chosen by **56%**, was **about the same**. The correct answer is the **smaller hospital**, because small samples vary more from the true rate, so a lopsided day is more likely with 15 births than with 45. Choosing the larger hospital confuses the absolute number of boys with how often an extreme proportion occurs.", "children": ["The common answer ignores how many births stand behind each day's percentage."]}, {"text": "This is why the **law of large numbers** matters: larger groups are more representative of the population, and people tend to expect small samples to be representative too."}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Lord and colleagues · myside bias in article evaluation", "researcher": "Lord and colleagues (1979)", "pages": [16], "blocks": [{"text": "Does an existing opinion change how the same evidence is evaluated? **Lord and colleagues (1979)** compared people who were **in favor of capital punishment** with people who were **opposed** to it. Both groups read the same article, and the researchers measured how each participant interpreted it.", "children": ["The only thing that differed between the groups was the readers' opinion, not the article."]}, {"text": "Each side saw the article as **favorable to its own position**, focusing on information that agreed with them and disregarding the rest.", "children": ["Identical evidence was read as support by opposite camps."]}, {"text": "This is **myside bias**: generating and evaluating evidence in a way that favors one's own opinions and attitudes. It is a form of **confirmation bias**, the tendency to seek information that fits a view and overlook information that argues against it."}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Hidden number rule · confirmation and diagnostic tests", "researcher": "", "pages": [17], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people look for evidence that could prove their own hypothesis wrong? In the **2, 4, 6 task**, participants were told that 2, 4, 6 fits a hidden rule. Nothing varied between groups. Each participant wrote their own sets of three numbers, received feedback on whether each set fit the experimenter's rule, and then proposed the rule.", "children": ["The task records what people choose to test, not just what they conclude."]}, {"text": "The most common hypothesis was **increasing intervals of two**. People mostly tested triples that fit that idea, so they kept hearing yes. The actual rule was simply **three numbers in increasing order**.", "children": ["Repeated yes feedback cannot separate the narrow rule from the broader one."]}, {"text": "Testing only what fits a hypothesis is **confirmation bias** and keeps a too-narrow belief alive. Trying to **disconfirm** the hypothesis, by testing triples that should not fit, would reveal that the belief is wrong."}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Jellybeans · favorable counts versus winning probability", "researcher": "", "pages": [20], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people choose by the number of winning items or by the probability of winning? Participants could earn **one dollar** for each red jellybean they drew, up to seven dollars, and before drawing they picked between two bowls. One bowl had **1 red bean out of 10**, and the other had **7 red beans out of 100**.", "children": ["Comparing counts and comparing ratios give opposite answers here."]}, {"text": "Most participants chose the **7-out-of-100 bowl**, even though 1 in 10 is a **10%** chance and 7 in 100 is only **7%**. The larger number of red beans looks like more chances, and it draws attention away from the total number of possible draws.", "children": ["The bigger count hides the bigger total."]}, {"text": "The choice was driven by considerations other than the actual probabilities of winning."}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Ritov and Baron · convoy rerouting and lives saved", "researcher": "Ritov and Baron (1999)", "pages": [22], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people choose the option that saves the most lives when it requires causing harm? **Ritov and Baron (1999)** described a convoy of food trucks heading to a refugee camp during a famine. Rerouting the convoy would save **1,000 people at a second camp**, but **100 people in the first camp would die**. The measure was whether participants chose to reroute.", "children": ["The net count favors rerouting, 1,000 minus 100 is 900 lives saved."]}, {"text": "Even so, **63%** did not choose to reroute, and some would keep the original route even if only **one person** would die after the change.", "children": ["Most refused despite the better net outcome."]}, {"text": "Reluctance to cause a loss can outweigh the larger number saved elsewhere, so choices are not simply about the biggest number."}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Tetlock and colleagues · taboo and tragic outcomes", "researcher": "Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green, and Lerner (2000)", "pages": [23, 24, 25], "blocks": [{"text": "How do taboo and tragic tradeoffs affect moral outrage? **Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green, and Lerner (2000)** crossed two kinds of tradeoff with two outcomes. In the **taboo** tradeoff, a hospital administrator must choose between saving Johnny's life, which costs one million dollars, and spending that money on other hospital needs. In the **tragic** tradeoff, two children need a liver and only one is available. Johnny was either saved or not saved, and participants rated moral outrage.", "children": ["A taboo tradeoff sets a sacred value against a secular benefit, while a tragic tradeoff sets two sacred values against each other."]}, {"text": "In the taboo tradeoff, **not saving Johnny produced much greater outrage** than saving him. In the tragic tradeoff, outrage was **similar** whether Johnny was saved or not, since either choice leaves another sacred concern unmet.", "children": ["The effect of the decision depends on the type of value conflict, which is an **interaction**."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Ginges and colleagues · payment, recognition, and sacred commitments", "researcher": "Ginges, Atran, Medin, and Shikaki (2007)", "pages": [26, 27, 28, 29], "blocks": [{"text": "How do people react to compromise deals over contested issues, and does money help? **Ginges, Atran, Medin, and Shikaki (2007)** surveyed **601 Israeli settlers, 535 Palestinian refugees, and 719 Palestinian students**. Each person responded to a **taboo** deal, a **Taboo+** deal that added one billion dollars a year for ten years, and a **symbolic** deal that added recognition and an apology. Participants were also classified as **moral absolutists** or not, and the researchers measured anger, disgust and support for violence.", "children": ["Moral absolutists treat an issue as sacred and reject compromise regardless of benefit."]}, {"text": "For moral absolutists, **adding money increased anger, disgust and support for violence**, while non-absolutists responded the other way. **Symbolic recognition** lowered anger or disgust and support for violence.", "children": ["Money can backfire when the issue is sacred, and symbolic reciprocity can help."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Iranian online study · sacred values, funds, sanctions, and energy results", "researcher": "Dehghani, Atran, and colleagues (2010); Dehghani, Iliev, and colleagues (2009)", "pages": [30, 31, 32, 33], "blocks": [{"text": "Do incentives or sanctions make Iranians who hold a sacred value more willing to give up the nuclear program? **Dehghani, Atran, and colleagues (2010)** and **Dehghani, Iliev, and colleagues (2009)** ran an online study in a **2 x 3 design**: program type (energy or weapons) by deal type. The **Taboo** deal was the baseline, **Taboo+** added 40 billion dollars, and **Taboo-** added the threat of sanctions. Participants rated personal approval, the anger they expected if the government approved, and whether the deal seemed misleading.", "children": ["A sacred-value response meant giving up the program should not be done no matter how great the consequences."]}, {"text": "Only about **14%** (energy) and **13%** (weapons) gave that response. In the energy condition, those participants showed **lower approval** with funds or sanctions than with the basic deal, and anticipated anger was **highest with funds**. Participants without a sacred value gave higher approval and lower anger, with little change across deals.", "children": ["For a sacred commitment, improving the financial terms does not make a deal more acceptable."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Kermer and colleagues · expected and actual emotions after a coin flip", "researcher": "Kermer and colleagues (2006)", "pages": [4], "blocks": [{"text": "**Kermer and colleagues (2006)** asked whether people can accurately predict how they will feel after a win or a loss. Participants first rated their baseline happiness and were given $5. They were then told that a coin flip would either win them another **$5** or lose them **$3**, and their predicted feelings before the flip were compared with their actual feelings afterward."}, {"text": "Beforehand, people expected losing $3 to drop their happiness by about **4 points** and winning $5 to raise it by about **2 points**. Afterward, the real drop from losing was only about **1 point**, and the real rise from winning was about **1.2 points**.", "children": ["The expected loss was about twice the expected gain, even though the loss was the smaller amount of money."]}, {"text": "People overestimated how bad a loss would feel because they ignore the **coping mechanisms** that get activated and soften it. These inaccurate **expected emotions** feed **risk aversion**."}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Simonsohn · cloudy days, admissions, and enrollment", "researcher": "Simonsohn (2007); Simonsohn (2009)", "pages": [5], "blocks": [{"text": "**Simonsohn** asked whether the weather, an **incidental** factor, carries over into important decisions. The manipulated factor was cloudy versus sunny days, either on the day of an admissions decision or on the day a student visited a campus."}, {"text": "**Simonsohn (2007)** analyzed university admissions decisions and found that applicants' academic attributes were weighted more heavily on **cloudy** days than on sunny days. **Simonsohn (2009)** looked at prospective students visiting a highly rated university and found that those who visited on a **cloudy** day were more likely to enroll.", "children": ["2007 is admissions decisions, and 2009 is enrollment by visitors."]}, {"text": "The weather has nothing to do with the applicant or the school, yet it changed important decisions, which shows that incidental emotions can shape choices."}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Lerner and colleagues · mood and highlighter prices", "researcher": "Lerner and colleagues (2004)", "pages": [6], "blocks": [{"text": "**Lerner and colleagues (2004)** asked whether a mood unrelated to a decision can change how much an object seems worth. Participants first watched a film clip that was **sad** (a person dying), **disgust** (a person using a dirty toilet), or **neutral** (fish at the Great Barrier Reef). They then set a selling price and a buying price for a set of highlighters."}, {"text": "For **selling**, neutral was about **$4.50**, disgust about **$2.70**, and sad about **$3.00**. For **buying**, neutral was about **$3.50**, disgust about **$3.00**, and sad about **$4.50**.", "children": ["Disgust lowered both prices, while sadness lowered the selling price but raised the buying price."]}, {"text": "The clips had nothing to do with the highlighters, so these were **incidental emotions**, and they still changed how much the same object seemed worth."}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Physicians · earlier cases and cesarean recommendations", "researcher": "", "pages": [7], "blocks": [{"text": "This study asked whether the **context** of earlier cases changes a decision about the very same case. Physicians judged one hypothetical cesarean test case in three contexts: alone first as the **control**, after four cases with **serious complications**, or after four **fairly routine** cases. The measure was whether they recommended a cesarean section for the test case."}, {"text": "**Significantly more doctors recommended a cesarean after reading the non-serious cases first.** The test case itself never changed, and only the cases that came before it did.", "children": ["The cases just considered changed how the same patient was judged."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Organ donation · opt-in and opt-out consent", "researcher": "", "pages": [8], "blocks": [{"text": "This comparison asked whether the way a choice is presented changes how many people become organ donors. In an **opt-in** procedure a person must take an active step to be a donor, while in an **opt-out** procedure a person is a donor unless they request otherwise. The measure was the consent rate in countries using each procedure."}, {"text": "Opt-in countries have consent rates **under 28 percent**, while opt-out countries have consent rates **over 99 percent**. Doing nothing makes a person a non-donor under opt-in but a donor under opt-out.", "children": ["The default, not the people's wishes alone, decides the result."]}, {"text": "This is the **status quo bias**, the tendency to do nothing when a decision is difficult."}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Tversky and Kahneman · the disease problem in gain and loss frames", "researcher": "Tversky and Kahneman (1981)", "pages": [9, 11, 12], "blocks": [{"text": "**Tversky and Kahneman (1981)** asked whether describing the same outcomes as gains or as losses changes risk-taking. A disease is expected to kill 600 people. In the **gain frame**, Program A saves 200 for certain, while Program B has a 1/3 chance that all 600 are saved and a 2/3 chance that none are saved. In the **loss frame**, Program C means 400 die for certain, while Program D has a 1/3 chance that no one dies and a 2/3 chance that all 600 die."}, {"text": "In the gain frame, **72 percent** chose A, the sure option, and 28 percent chose B. In the loss frame, **78 percent** chose D, the gamble, and 22 percent chose C. Yet **A and C are the same outcome**, since saving 200 of 600 means 400 die, and **B and D are the same gamble**.", "children": ["Only the wording changed, and most people switched from the sure option to the gamble."]}, {"text": "This is the **framing effect**: gains push people toward **risk aversion**, and losses push them toward **risk taking**."}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Sanfey and colleagues · the ultimatum game and the anterior insula", "researcher": "Sanfey and colleagues (2003)", "pages": [14, 15], "blocks": [{"text": "**Sanfey and colleagues (2003)** asked whether emotion and brain activity explain why people reject unfair offers that cost them money. In the **ultimatum game**, a proposer splits money and the responder either accepts, so the money is divided as offered, or rejects, so neither player gets anything. Offers were $5/$5, $7/$3, $8/$2 or $9/$1, and each participant played 20 games, 10 with a human partner and 10 with a computer partner. The researchers recorded acceptance or rejection and activation of the **right anterior insula**."}, {"text": "$5/$5 and $7/$3 offers were accepted almost always. An $8/$2 offer was accepted about **53 percent** of the time from a human versus **85 percent** from a computer, and a $9/$1 offer about **37 percent** versus **68 percent**. More right anterior insula activation meant rejecting more offers.", "children": ["Participants were angrier at an unfair human than at an unfair computer."]}, {"text": "**Utility theory** says to accept any offer, because any money beats nothing, but people reject and give up money. Emotion, here anger at unfairness, is important in decision making."}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Belief bias · judging invalid believable conclusions", "researcher": "", "pages": [18], "blocks": [{"text": "This research asked whether a believable conclusion makes people accept a syllogism as valid. Participants judged **invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions**, and the measure was whether they called them valid."}, {"text": "**71 percent** judged these invalid syllogisms as valid. This is **belief bias**: people judge by whether the conclusion sounds true instead of whether it follows from the premises.", "children": ["Sounding right is not the same as following."]}, {"text": "Take *All of the students are tired; Some tired people are irritable; therefore Some of the students are irritable*. Every statement sounds fine, but the conclusion does not follow, so the syllogism is **not valid**."}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Conditional syllogisms · four forms and accuracy", "researcher": "", "pages": [22, 23, 24, 25], "blocks": [{"text": "This research asked which kinds of if-then syllogisms people judge correctly. All four forms start with **If p, then q**, and they differ in the second premise: **p** (modus ponens), **not q** (modus tollens), **q**, and **not p**. Participants judged each form as valid or invalid."}, {"text": "**Modus ponens** was judged correctly **97 percent** of the time, **modus tollens** 60 percent, and each of the two invalid forms, **syllogisms 3 and 4**, only **40 percent**. Both valid forms were judged correctly more often than both invalid ones.", "children": ["Modus tollens is just as valid as modus ponens, yet much harder."]}, {"text": "Modus ponens and modus tollens are valid, and syllogisms 3 and 4 are not."}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Wason four-card problem · abstract and concrete versions", "researcher": "", "pages": [26, 27, 28], "blocks": [{"text": "This research asked whether using real-world items makes conditional reasoning easier. In the **abstract version**, the cards read E, K, 4 and 7, and the rule was that a vowel on one side means an even number on the other. In the **concrete version**, the cards read Beer, Soda, 16 years old and 21 years old, and the rule was that drinking beer means being 21 years old. Participants chose which cards to turn over."}, {"text": "In the abstract version, **46 percent** chose the 4, which is an error, and only **7 percent** chose the correct 7. In the concrete version, **73 percent** answered correctly.", "children": ["The correct cards are E and 7 in the abstract version, and beer and 16 years old in the concrete version."]}, {"text": "Following the **falsification principle**, you look for cases that could break the rule, so the 4 and the 21 cannot help. **Permission schemas** in familiar rules make this easier."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Phonological Similarity Effect", "researcher": "Conrad (1964); Murray (1968)", "pages": [1], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people turn silently read items into a sound-based code? **Conrad (1964)** and **Murray (1968)** tested this with lists of letters that either sounded alike or sounded different, and people had to click the letters back in the order shown. On some trials they also said 'one, two, three, four' out loud, which is **articulatory suppression**, and on others they stayed quiet.", "children": ["Sound-alike lists are harder to hold in order, even when read silently."]}, {"text": "**Similar-sounding lists were recalled worse**, even though nobody spoke them. When suppression occupied the speech system on visually presented lists, the cost disappeared. This shows that people **recode what they see into a speech-based form**, which is the basis of the **phonological loop**.", "children": ["The mix-up is about similar sound, not similar look. A visual code would instead confuse look-alikes such as E and F."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Neighborhood Size Effect", "researcher": "Roodenrys et al. (2002)", "pages": [2], "blocks": [{"text": "Does what you already know about words in long-term memory change how well you hold them in short-term memory? **Roodenrys et al. (2002)** compared words from **large neighborhoods**, like cat, with words from **small neighborhoods**, like hippopotamus, and measured how well people recalled the words in order.", "children": ["A neighbor differs by one letter or one sound. A word that only means the same thing is not a neighbor."]}, {"text": "**Large neighborhood words were recalled better** in short-term memory tasks. So long-term knowledge of words helps short-term recall, and **memory span is not a pure measure of short-term capacity**."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Brown-Peterson Task", "researcher": "Peterson and Peterson (1959)", "pages": [3], "blocks": [{"text": "What happens to short-term memory when rehearsal is blocked? In the **Brown-Peterson task**, **Peterson and Peterson (1959)** had people study consonants and then count backwards for varying lengths of time before recalling them. The counting kept them from rehearsing.", "children": ["The longer the distraction, the worse the recall."]}, {"text": "Because counting is so unlike remembering letters, the researchers judged interference an unlikely cause and concluded that short-term memory loses information through **decay** unless it is rehearsed. This challenged the view, following McGeoch's (1932) critique, that forgetting is due to interference.", "children": ["Decay is the researchers' conclusion, and Keppel and Underwood (1962) are cited for an alternative based on interference."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Modality Effect", "researcher": "", "pages": [4], "blocks": [{"text": "Is memory better when a list is heard than when it is read? The **modality effect** compares lists that were heard with lists read silently, using digits in random order, and looks at memory for the last one or two items.", "children": ["The advantage is for the last items of the list, not the whole list."]}, {"text": "Heard lists gave **better memory for the final items**, and lip-read or silently mouthed lists show it too. The favored explanation (**Neath and Surprenant, 2003**) is that hearing adds information about how the list sounded, which helps the final items. The effect strongly influenced ideas about **sensory memory**, a back-up store of raw, unanalyzed sensory input."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Partial Report", "researcher": "Sperling (1960)", "pages": [5], "blocks": [{"text": "People can report only a small part of a brief display. Is that limit in perception or in recall? **Sperling (1960)** varied whether a cue telling observers which part to report came right after the display or after a delay, and measured how much of the cued part they reported.", "children": ["The limit is in reporting, not in what people saw."]}, {"text": "With an **immediate cue**, observers reported the cued part almost perfectly, so a **sensory (iconic) store** briefly held nearly all of the display. With a **delayed cue**, performance fell toward the usual limit. The store is very short-lived, so the real limit is the time needed to report."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Position Error Effect", "researcher": "", "pages": [6], "blocks": [{"text": "How are order errors in recall patterned? Nothing was compared across groups here. Instead, each recalled letter's placed position was compared with its true position in the list."}, {"text": "Items were **usually placed in the correct position**, and when they were not, the errors were **systematic**: the item landed in an **adjacent position**. So people keep approximate position information.", "children": ["Order errors are not random, and scoring only percent correct hides this pattern."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Mental Rotation", "researcher": "Shepard and Metzler (1971)", "pages": [7], "blocks": [{"text": "Do mental images behave like real images? Self-reports of imagery are unreliable, because it is impossible to tell whether the images differ or only the descriptions. So researchers use tasks that require imagery. **Shepard and Metzler (1971)** showed two block shapes, one turned relative to the other, and people judged whether they were the same shape or mirror images.", "children": ["Self-reports of imagery cannot be trusted, so the task itself must require imagery."]}, {"text": "The **larger the turn, the longer the response**, at a constant rate. People were mentally rotating one shape, which suggests **mental images are much like real images**. This influenced theories of mental representation."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Typical Reasoning", "researcher": "Tversky and Kahneman (1983); Shafir, Smith, and Osherson (1990)", "pages": [8], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people judge probability by the rules of probability or by heuristics? **Tversky and Kahneman (1983)** and **Shafir, Smith, and Osherson (1990)** had people rate how likely descriptions of a person were to fit single professions or activities versus combinations of them.", "children": ["A combination can never be more likely than either part alone."]}, {"text": "People sometimes rated a combination as **more likely** than one of its parts, which is the **conjunction fallacy**. This points to **heuristics** that trade accuracy for speed: usually good enough, but sometimes wrong."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Wason Selection", "researcher": "Wason and Shapiro (1971)", "pages": [9], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people test abstract rules as well as familiar ones? **Wason and Shapiro (1971)** compared abstract rules, with letters and numbers, to thematic rules, like borrowing a car, and recorded which cards people chose to turn over.", "children": ["The 3 can only confirm the rule, while the 7 could falsify it."]}, {"text": "For the rule 'If a card has a D on one side, it has a 3 on the other', most people chose **D and 3** instead of the better test, **D and 7**. **Thematic rules were much easier**. With relevant experience people test a rule well, while abstract rules force them to use logic alone."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Decision Making", "researcher": "Kahneman and Tversky (1982); Tversky and Kahneman", "pages": [10], "blocks": [{"text": "Can wording or context change choices between options that are otherwise equivalent? **Kahneman and Tversky (1982)** summarized many such **framing effects**. In classic framing research (Tversky and Kahneman), the same choice is presented with gain wording or with loss wording, and people choose which option they prefer.", "children": ["The situations are otherwise equivalent, so only the wording differs."]}, {"text": "**Gain wording led to the sure option** and **loss wording to the gamble**. So choices can follow the framing and not the substance."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Monty Hall", "researcher": "", "pages": [11], "blocks": [{"text": "Do people have good intuition about the probability of combined events? In the **Monty Hall 3 Door problem**, you pick one of three doors, and the host, who always plays fair this way, opens a no-prize door. The question is whether you should stay or switch.", "children": ["The two remaining doors are not equally likely."]}, {"text": "Many people think it makes no difference, which is incorrect. **Switching wins about two-thirds of the time** and staying about one-third. Your first pick keeps its 1 in 3 chance, so the 2 in 3 held by the other two doors moves to the one remaining door."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Risky Decisions", "researcher": "Kahneman and Tversky", "pages": [12], "blocks": [{"text": "Do risky choices depend on whether outcomes are seen as gains or losses? In research on **prospect theory**, **Kahneman and Tversky** had people pick between a guaranteed outcome and a gamble, where the guaranteed outcome could be a gain or a loss.", "children": ["Losses lead to gambling, not caution."]}, {"text": "With gains, people **avoided risk** and took the sure thing. With losses, people **sought risk** and took the gamble. So decisions about risk depend on how outcomes are perceived, not only on the objective odds."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "German & Defeyter (2000): Immunity to functional fixedness in young children", "researcher": "German and Defeyter (2000)", "pages": [13], "blocks": [{"text": "Do young children get stuck on an object's usual function, which is **functional fixedness**? **German and Defeyter (2000)** compared children who had first seen a box used as a container with children who had not, and measured how long they took to offer the box as a support. They tested 5-, 6- and 7-year-olds."}, {"text": "Children aged 6 and 7 were **slower** after seeing the box as a container. The 5-year-olds were not slowed and were even faster than the older children. Younger children seem to have a more fluid idea of function, so one demonstrated use did not lock the object in.", "children": ["Younger children did better here, not worse."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Leist et al. (2022): Irrelevant speech impairs serial recall of verbal but not spatial items", "researcher": "Leist et al. (2022)", "pages": [14], "blocks": [{"text": "Does speech that people are told to ignore hurt only verbal items, or spatial items too? **Leist and colleagues (2022)** had third graders and adults recall the order of verbal or spatial items while hearing quiet, steady-state or changing-state speech."}, {"text": "Speech **hurt verbal recall but not spatial recall**, in both children and adults. Changing-state speech hurt more than steady-state speech, but only for verbal items. The effect is specific to verbal material, which fits **phonological interference** better than attention-capture or changing-state accounts.", "children": ["The irrelevant speech effect depends on the material being verbal."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Morrison & Richmond (2020): Offloading items from memory", "researcher": "Morrison and Richmond (2020)", "pages": [15], "blocks": [{"text": "Who offloads items from memory, and does it help? **Morrison and Richmond (2020)** studied **cognitive offloading**, using actions like writing things down to reduce mental demands (Risko and Gilbert, 2016). A no-choice block allowed memory only, while a choice block allowed pen and paper, with lists of different lengths."}, {"text": "**Offloading improved memory**, especially at high loads, and people offloaded more as load grew. Low-capacity people were expected to offload more, but **memory ability did not predict who offloaded**. So offloading may be a compensatory strategy across abilities."}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Hoover & Healy (2019): The Bat-and-Ball Problem", "researcher": "Hoover and Healy (2019)", "pages": [16], "blocks": [{"text": "In the **bat-and-ball problem** (Frederick, 2005), a bat and ball cost $1.10 and the bat costs $1.00 more. The intuitive answer of 10 cents is wrong; the answer is 5 cents. Kahneman and Frederick (2002) called the error **unconscious attribute substitution**, and De Neys and colleagues (2013) found wrong reasoners were less confident than on a control version."}, {"text": "**Hoover and Healy (2019)** tested whether people unconsciously ignore 'more than', comparing the standard problem with a control version without it. They measured confidence and later recall and recognition of the wording. **Wrong reasoners mostly remembered 'more than'**, so it was not simply overlooked.", "children": ["Substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause, so the authors prefer the term **error sensitivity**."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Giuliani et al. (2023): The joint effect of framing and defaults on choice behavior", "researcher": "Giuliani et al. (2023)", "pages": [17], "blocks": [{"text": "What happens when the **framing effect** and the **default effect** act together? **Giuliani and colleagues (2023)** varied gain versus loss wording and also whether there was no default, a sure default or a risky default, in life-or-death and money scenarios. They measured whether people chose the sure or the risky option."}, {"text": "A **risky default raised risk taking**, a sure default did not raise risk aversion, and the framing and default effects were **additive**. In an exploratory question asked after the choice, people who thought the default was most rational followed it most.", "children": ["That belief question came after the choice, so it may be a justification rather than a cause."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Chein et al. (2011) and Albert, Chein & Steinberg (2013): Peers and the adolescent brain", "researcher": "Chein et al. (2011); Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013)", "pages": [18], "blocks": [{"text": "Why do adolescents take more risks with peers around? **Chein and colleagues (2011)** scanned adolescents, young adults and adults playing a driving game either alone or watched by friends, measuring risky driving choices and activity in reward and cognitive-control regions."}, {"text": "Adolescents **took more risks when watched**, with greater reward-region activity, while cognitive-control regions did not change with peers. The authors concluded the effect works mainly through **reward circuitry**. **Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013)** argue that adolescents reason about risk like adults in the lab, and the peer context is what raises risk taking.", "children": ["The peer context changes behavior, not the ability to reason about risk."]}]}];
